---
import LegalLayout from '@/layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/consts';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Advertising & affiliate disclosure"
  description="How DevToolSDK makes money, which links are affiliate links, and the specific things we refuse to do — including selling placement or changing a score."
  path="/disclosure"
  lead="Two revenue sources, both disclosed. Neither has ever changed a score, and this page explains the controls that make that claim checkable."
>
  <h2 id="summary">The short version</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} makes money from display advertising and from affiliate links. We do not sell
    reviews, rankings, placement in a comparison table, or the outcome of a verdict. If we ever
    did, this page would say so, because the alternative is lying to you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="advertising">Display advertising</h2>
  <p>
    We serve display ads through Google AdSense. Google sells the inventory and places the ads.
    We cannot see which advertisers bought space on a given page, and advertisers cannot see what
    we are writing or influence it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That separation is the point. An advertiser who is unhappy about a review has no lever to pull
    — they are not our customer, Google is. Every ad slot on this site is labelled
    "Advertisement", and we deliberately keep no ads above the fold, none on the contact page, and
    the ad load below the level where it starts degrading page performance.
  </p>

  <h2 id="affiliate">Affiliate links</h2>
  <p>
    Some links to the tools we review are affiliate links. If you click one and later sign up or
    buy, we may receive a commission at no additional cost to you.
  </p>
  <p>Specifically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Affiliate links carry <code>rel="sponsored nofollow"</code> in the page source, so they are
      machine-identifiable, not just disclosed in prose.
    </li>
    <li>
      A minority of the tools we cover have affiliate programmes at all. Most do not, and we
      review them identically.
    </li>
    <li>
      We write the review and assign the scores <strong>before</strong> anyone checks whether a
      programme exists. The commercial question is deliberately asked last.
    </li>
    <li>
      Commission rates vary between vendors and are never taken into account when ordering a
      comparison or writing a verdict.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-we-refuse">What we refuse to do</h2>
  <p>
    This list exists so you can hold us to it. We do not:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Publish sponsored reviews, paid reviews, or vendor-written copy under our byline</li>
    <li>Sell placement in a comparison table or a "featured partner" slot</li>
    <li>Change a score because a vendor asked, complained, or threatened</li>
    <li>Give a vendor advance sight of a verdict in time to respond publicly</li>
    <li>Accept payment for a link, or add an affiliate link to a tool we did not independently choose to cover</li>
    <li>Remove a negative review in exchange for anything</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Vendors get exactly one privilege: a factual-accuracy pass on the draft. They can tell us we
    described a pricing tier incorrectly. They cannot change a conclusion.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-scores-work">How the scores are produced</h2>
  <p>
    Every score on this site is a weighted average of five fixed criteria — developer experience
    (30%), total cost of ownership (25%), operational maturity (20%), ecosystem and lock-in (15%)
    and trajectory (10%). The weights do not change between reviews, which is what makes two
    DevToolSDK scores comparable to each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full process, including how long we run a tool before scoring it, is on our
    <a href="/about#methodology">methodology page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ftc">Regulatory note</h2>
  <p>
    This disclosure is made in accordance with the United States Federal Trade Commission's
    16 CFR Part 255 guidance on endorsements and testimonials, and with equivalent advertising
    standards in the EU and UK requiring commercial relationships to be disclosed clearly and
    conspicuously.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">If you think we broke this</h2>
  <p>
    Tell us. Email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> or use the
    <a href="/contact">contact form</a> and select "Report a factual error". If you are right, we
    publish the correction and say what happened. A disclosure policy nobody can challenge is
    decoration.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
